>  
Six years of work, countless hours in the library and the lab, hunched over your
laptop, poring over old manuscripts or departmental paperwork...and now it’s
almost over. Your advisor has given you a deadline, and you need to defend your
thesis by the end of the year, or leave with nothing to show for it.

Your data is promising. The experimental results are fantastic, the p-values are
significant, and you’ve gotten a couple publications out of the process. But if
you want to be the first ever PhD in Forensic Necromancy, you need more than lab
results. You need something the world can’t ignore.

Which means you need human remains in situ, and the older the better. The last
of your advisor’s grant funding got you to Nineveh just after midnight. The rest
is up to you.

[0m[1mA Murder in Nineveh
[0mSubmitted in partial fulfillment of the requirements for the degree of Doctor of
Philosophy in
[0m[1mForensic Necromancy
[0m
A Historical Mystery by Daniel M. Stelzer.
Release 0. Serial number DEBUG.
Dialog Interactive Debugger (dgdebug) version 1c/01-dev. Library version 1.2.0.
Debugging extension 1.1. Draconis extensions: Automap 1.2, Suggestion Bars 1.0,
Puzzle Prompts 1.0, and Versatile Linking 1.0.

[0m[1mTell Nabi Yunus[0m
No hiking through the Iraqi desert for you: the dig site is right in the heart
of Mosul, the mound known as Prophet Jonah’s Hill. There’s supposed to be a very
nice bakery across the street, but it wasn’t open at 2 in the morning; you had
to make do with a box of cookies from the supermarket on the way over.

But you made it. They say the field crew came across more bones today, and
that’s exactly what you need.

[0m[4mThis game is played by clicking links or typing in commands. To move around, for
example, click or type a compass direction: [0m[4m[50m<southwest>[0m[4m ([0m[4m[50m<sw>[0m[4m) or [0m[4m[50m<south>[0m[4m ([0m[4m[50m<s>[0m[4m).
