command: w
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
> w
You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts...

[0m[4m“Attention, citizens of Babylon!” A new voice cuts through the mid-morning
chatter. You set down your bucket with a sigh, and give your brother a nudge. A
stranger has stepped up on the edge of the fountain to bellow to the crowd.

“His Majesty Siin-ahhe-eriba, Great King, Mighty King, King of the World, King
of Assyria, King of the Four Quarters, the capable shepherd, favorite of the
Great Gods, guardian of truth, he who loves justice, renders assistance, offers
aid to the weak, and strives after good deeds, the Perfect Man, the virile
warrior, foremost of all rulers, the bridle that tames the rebellious, the one
who strikes his enemies with lightning—”

He cuts off abruptly, glancing around. The tone of the crowd has become
decidedly hostile. He swallows, clears his throat, and starts again. “His
Majesty, in his wisdom, has selected an heir. After his passing, he will not be
succeeded by his eldest son, Urad-Mullissi, but by his chosen crown prince,
Asshur-ahu-iddina.”

[0mThat[0m[4m gets the crowd’s attention. Sharhiya stands up, squinting at the messenger.
“Asshur-ahu-iddina? He’s not even a son of the queen, is he?”

You shake your head. “No. Just a concubine. And he’s [0myoung[0m[4m, too. But they say
his mother is a clever one. Maybe he takes after her.”

He shushes you, and you go quiet as the messenger continues. “In one month’s
time, all the lords of Babylonia will assemble at the Great Palace in Nineveh to
renew your oaths of fealty. May His Maj—”

That’s when a vegetable hits him across the face. “Heir or no heir, he’s no true
king until he’s crowned by Marduk!” The messenger tries to get back to his feet,
but the whole audience is shouting now. “Send soldiers if you want! What’ll they
do that they haven’t done already?” “Make him remember what happened to the last
crown prince who set foot in Babylon!”

“Come on!” Sharhiya grabs you by the shoulder, and the pair of you turn and run.
There’s no sign of the messenger now behind the crowd of angry Babylonians.
[0m
